  "text": "Interviewers rarely ask a data scientist to train a network. They ask you to explain one: what a single neuron computes, how the error travels back through the layers, and why, in a deep enough stack, it fades to almost nothing on the way. Of the fifteen questions in this video, twelve get opened up on networks small enough to show every number, worked by hand in NumPy. Three quick ones close it. The smallest piece first.\n\nQuestion one. What does a single artificial neuron compute? A weighted sum of its inputs, plus a bias, passed through an activation function. These three inputs, times their weights, plus the bias, come to one point four. A sigmoid turns that into point eight. Leave out the activation, and the neuron is just linear regression. Question two. Why do networks need non-linear activations? Because two linear layers are only one. This input goes through two weight matrices and comes out as one, minus eight. Multiply the matrices together first, and that single matrix gives one, minus eight as well, for every input. Ten layers would collapse the same way. Put an activation between them that zeroes the negatives, and the answer becomes three, minus six. Now depth adds something. So, which activation?",
